{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoComplexPentagon"],"declarations":16,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_eq_pi_sq_div_two'","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_eq_pi_sq_div_two'","kind":"theorem","line":366,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L366-L374","doc":"The flattened identity with the **complex** slot fixed: for any `v` off both\ncuts and any real `a ∈ (0,1)`, `Σ flatRogers = π²/2`. This is\n`flatPentagonSum_eq_pi_sq_div_two` read through the symmetry, and it is the base\npoint a second transport would start from. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_swap","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_swap","kind":"theorem","line":362,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L362-L364","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_symm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_symm","kind":"theorem","line":353,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L353-L360","doc":"**The flattened sum is symmetric in its two variables**, like the unflattened\none: `x ↔ y` permutes the five triples `(argument, Λ, Λ')` rather than changing\nthem (`1 ↔ 2`, `3 ↔ 5`, `4` fixed). So transport in the *second* variable is the\nsame theorem — which is what reaching a pair with both entries non-real needs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.not_pentagonRegular_of_prod_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.not_pentagonRegular_of_prod_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":335,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L335-L351","doc":"**The pentagon is not regular at the 5₂ conjugate pair.**\n\nIf the third root `c` is real in `(0,1)` — as `y₃ = 0.4301597…` is — then\n`1 − ab` is the negative real `(c−1)/c` and `PentagonRegular b a` fails outright.\nNo choice of region reaches it; the configuration is on the boundary. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.not_mem_slitPlane_ofReal_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.not_mem_slitPlane_ofReal_neg","kind":"theorem","line":330,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L330-L333","doc":"A negative real is off the slit plane. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.one_sub_mul_of_prod_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.one_sub_mul_of_prod_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":325,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L325-L328","doc":"`c·(1 − ab) = c − 1` whenever `abc = 1` — one line, and it is the whole\nobstruction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_eq_pi_sq_div_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_eq_pi_sq_div_two","kind":"theorem","line":283,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L283-L293","doc":"The unconditional one-variable case, flattened: for real `a ∈ (0,1)` and any\n`w` off both cuts. Here it coincides with\n`FiveTwoComplexPentagon.pentagonSum_eq_pi_sq_div_two`, since the branch\nconditions happen to hold; the flattened statement is the one that survives when\nthey do not. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_eq_pi_sq_div_two_of_region","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_eq_pi_sq_div_two_of_region","kind":"theorem","line":263,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L263-L281","doc":"**The flattened five-term identity on a regular region reaching the reals.**\n\n    Σ flatRogers = π²/2\n\nwith `y` real in `(0,1)`, on any open connected set of `x` that stays regular and\ncontains a real point of `(0,1)`. The hypothesis is regularity **only** —\ndifferentiability of the five terms — with no branch condition to check anywhere,\nwhich is precisely what `FiveTwoComplexPentagon` could not say.\n\n⚠ It is still a region hypothesis, so it does not reach `(y₁, ȳ₁)` and does not\ntouch `Vol(5₂)`. **The 5₂ sum rule is PROVED**: `FiveTwoComplexPentagon.fiveTwo_saddle_sum_rule`\nand `fiveTwoSaddleSumRule_holds`, sorry-free, standard axioms. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_ofReal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_ofReal","kind":"theorem","line":256,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L256-L261","doc":"On the positive real square the flattened sum is `π²/2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_eq_pentagonSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_eq_pentagonSum","kind":"theorem","line":239,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L239-L254","doc":"Where the branch conditions do hold, flattening changes nothing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_eq_of_region","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum_eq_of_region","kind":"theorem","line":225,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L225-L237","doc":"The flattened pentagon is constant on any open connected region of regular\npoints. **No branch condition anywhere** — contrast\n`FiveTwoComplexPentagon.pentagonSum_eq_of_region`, which needed five at the base\npoint. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.hasDerivAt_flatPentagonSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.hasDerivAt_flatPentagonSum","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L128-L221","doc":"**The flattened pentagon's derivative vanishes — no branch hypotheses.**\n\nCompare `FiveTwoComplexPentagon.hasDerivAt_pentagonSum`, which needs five. Every\none of them has been absorbed into the definition of `flatRogers`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatPentagonSum","kind":"def","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L113-L126","doc":"The pentagon sum, flattened: each composite argument carries its **expansion**\nas its logarithm rather than the principal branch. The first two terms are\n`cRogersL` unchanged (`flatRogers_self`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.hasDerivAt_flatRogers","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.hasDerivAt_flatRogers","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L73-L109","doc":"**The derivative of the flattened term is `rogersDeriv`, exactly.**\n\nThe two hypotheses on `Λ`, `Λ'` are that they differentiate *like* `log f` and\n`log(1−f)` — which for the pentagon's expansions is a rational-function identity,\nnot a branch choice. Note `log f` still appears in `flatRogers`, and its\nderivative cancels against the `Λ'` term; that cancellation is the whole\nmechanism. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatRogers_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatRogers_self","kind":"theorem","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L67-L71","doc":"With the true logarithms in the slots, flattening is the identity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatRogers","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.flatRogers","kind":"def","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/FiveTwoFlattenedPentagon.lean#L62-L65","doc":"**The flattened Rogers dilogarithm.** `Λ`, `Λ'` stand in for `log z` and\n`log(1−z)`; when they *are* those logarithms this is `cRogersL z`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}